findings.claim:
The new SQL parser and seal surface has no authored refusal control. Missing cases include:
- raw SHA mismatch against the publication or decision entry;
- byte ceilings;
- nested duplicate keys and depth over 32;
- unknown quickOrderScope or offer keys;
- absent or ambiguous binding;
- ProductControl mismatch;
- non-canonical domains or country;
- the R2-16 named BEFORE INSERT seal after a header;
- UPDATE/DELETE/TRUNCATE on scope_inputs, scope_members, activations and revocations;
- sealed-row tamper ('sealed scope rows changed');
- header binding mismatch.
The successor's coverage and remaining lists do not mention this absence.

R3 calls for closed row-kind, nullness and format constraints and 'all five ordered ... references'. Missing are:
- grammar or length checks on domain, country, offering_variant_id, product_identifier and variant_identifier;
- for lot and document rows, a requirement that at least one parent identity is non-null;
- required keys in header provenance;
- a carrier artifacts CHECK beyond length 5 (element shape and order are unchecked until derive).
Publish is the sole writer and verify recomputes the digest, so this is precision.

Derive runs three quadratic scans:
- each binding re-scans the whole bindings array for duplicates (:810);
- each offer scans the bindings (:814);
- each offer scans the configuration offers (:820-822).
At 4096 rows this is tens of millions of jsonb element visits inside publish and activation, while platform DML on all 12 IN relations stalls. R3's cost model leaves this to measurement.

verdicts.note:
Confirmed. Grep finds:
- no verifier statement that writes authority_scope_members or currentness_scope_inputs other than staging (mjs:909-915);
- only two reads (mjs:1335, :1352);
- no expectation of 'sealed scope insertion refused', 'raw input byte binding mismatch', 'scope JSON', 'binding identity invalid', 'Product Control', 'domain set noncanonical', 'sealed scope rows changed' or 'scope header binding invalid'.
The established pattern for history refusal (the revisions/head U/D/T controls at mjs:2040-2050) has no counterpart for the four new tables or for the R2-16 named seal. BINDINGS_SUCCESSOR coverage (:725-778) lists only the 28 QC/MP/CR cases, and remaining[] (:764-777) does not disclose this gap. As things stand, dropping the seal or the unique-keys predicate would turn no control red.

